[x] Decide how an agent authenticates on the dev pod $DECIDED 2026-10-08 by Darrion: the dedicated token approach. Was: the pod holds no durable secrets and provider auth stays on the controller, but a harness running on the pod needs provider auth. Recommendation: a dedicated token (like agent-sandbox's claude_token from claude setup-token), passed at VM start as a systemd credential so it lives only in /run, never in the image or on disk, and gone on reboot or wipe. Alternative: the harness stays on the desktop and drives the pod over SSH, which keeps auth off the pod entirely but means I am not really running there.$ !2 ^2026-10-08T16:10 %2026-10-08T16:16 #01a11dc8-101a-751e-ae45-a70b3b660cc1
[x] Build the dev-pod image and boot it in a disposable VM with root SSH $Stock path first: disposable signing keys from mkosi genkey in a private 0700 dir, scripts/mkosi-arch dev-pod build, boot with mkosi vm / systemd-vmspawn, root key via stock systemd credential ssh.authorized_keys.root. Check first why earlier candidates were built in a separate builder VM (Fedora SELinux relabeling, or desktop isolation?) and whether that still applies to Arch. Bounded resources, owned cleanup, keys removed afterwards. 2026-10-08: the earlier builder VM was a RAM workaround (desktop had 5.8 GiB free, 6.5 needed; history doc, Remote build approval), not an isolation rule. First attempt: unprivileged mkosi 27.1 build on the desktop in a 5G/800% user scope, disposable keys in a private 0700 dir under ~/.cache/personal-os-validation.$ !2 ^2026-10-08T16:10 %2026-10-08T16:36 #01a11dc8-100c-7431-805f-1e589a65ea81
[x] Add the agent harness to the dev-pod profile $Arch images have no agent CLI yet; add only what the task needs (claude, pi), following the image's existing package conventions. Per the auth decision, also update mkosi.profiles/dev-pod/mkosi.conf's comment ('Remote authenticated controllers execute work here; no agent auto-start/login'): the agent now runs on the pod with a runtime-only token credential.$ !3 ^2026-10-08T16:10 %2026-10-08T22:42 #01a11dc8-1029-74fa-9963-11180d7f7709
[x] Run one root-agent task on the dev pod and harvest it $The proof: the assistant works from the pod as root on a real task; changes come back to the desktop by harvest, since the pod cannot push.$ !3 ^2026-10-08T16:10 %2026-10-08T22:42 #01a11dc8-1038-74a7-a34a-fb724a968444
[x] Decide where the persistent dev pod runs and how it is reached $DECIDED 2026-10-08 by Darrion: a persistent VM on the desktop, as an experiment running the facilitator directly before flashing real hardware. Was: where does the persistent dev pod run? Recommendation: start as a persistent VM on the desktop (no new hardware, proven today; about 7 GiB RAM free, so 4 GiB guest), move to the separate dev machine from meta-analysis DECISIONS when it exists. Harvest: the desktop fetches from the pod (no push credentials on the pod). The proof ran as an ephemeral desktop VM.$ !2 ^2026-10-08T22:42 %2026-10-08T23:01 #01a11f2e-c53a-76df-815d-c305065ed9d2
[x] Run the facilitator's own session inside a persistent desktop VM $Experiment before flashing. Non-ephemeral mkosi vm: state persists until the next build, which is a wipe by design. Darrion reaches the session with ssh + tmux on the pod; repos arrive by push and leave by desktop fetch. Measure against the handover objective: wake-up cost and repeats on the pod vs the desktop. 2026-10-09: running as user unit dev-pod.service (mkosi vm, non-ephemeral, 4G/4 CPU, loopback 127.0.0.1:42222). Claude Code in tmux session 'facilitator'; assistant repo handed over (4107010). gh token sees public repos only until its repository access is widened. Outcome 2026-10-09: session 6 ran the facilitator on the pod (clearhead missing at first; it built one, and the desktop installed the release). Darrion then decided the facilitator stays on the desktop until it has its own device; the pod is a workbox. VM stopped with nothing unpushed; tokens gone with it.$ !2 ^2026-10-08T23:01 %2026-10-09T01:18 #01a11f41-09b6-72d3-9bf4-eea8c0cf5910
